WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.534
Let's actually build something.

2
00:00:02.534 --> 00:00:08.638
I gave Claude Code a fresh project and one instruction: build a task tracker, on MetaObjects.

3
00:00:10.038 --> 00:00:23.928
By the end, you'll have this: a working API serving real data from a typed stack — database schema, Zod validation, and CRUD routes, all generated from one model.

4
00:00:23.928 --> 00:00:26.329
Here's how, start to finish.

5
00:00:27.729 --> 00:00:34.833
First, what we're building — a task tracker: projects, and tasks with a status and a due date.

6
00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:39.216
All you need is Node and one package, the MetaObjects CLI.

7
00:00:39.216 --> 00:00:41.786
Everything else, Claude Code does.

8
00:00:43.186 --> 00:00:45.834
No models, no setup — just the ask.

9
00:00:45.834 --> 00:00:48.482
Here's how the build actually goes.

10
00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:51.567
Step one is always meta init.

11
00:00:51.567 --> 00:00:56.389
It scaffolds your model directory and your config — and an empty generators folder.

12
00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:59.817
Empty on purpose: nothing is generated until you choose it.

13
00:00:59.817 --> 00:01:02.374
But init does something more important, too.

14
00:01:03.774 --> 00:01:07.542
It drops six skills and an AGENTS file right into your repo.

15
00:01:07.542 --> 00:01:16.776
Your agent now knows the MetaObjects workflow — write the model, decide what to generate, generate it, and verify — before it writes a single line.

16
00:01:16.776 --> 00:01:18.912
You're not teaching it a new tool.

17
00:01:18.912 --> 00:01:20.859
The init step already did that.

18
00:01:22.259 --> 00:01:25.162
So Claude writes the model — one readable file.

19
00:01:25.162 --> 00:01:30.722
A Project, a Task with a status and a due date, the relationship between them, and a grid.

20
00:01:30.722 --> 00:01:36.713
And notice the validator sitting right on the title — child metadata that travels with the model.

21
00:01:36.713 --> 00:01:38.813
That's the entire source of truth.

22
00:01:40.213 --> 00:01:42.427
Now, which code does this app need?

23
00:01:42.427 --> 00:01:46.222
MetaObjects doesn't decide that, so Claude asks the catalog.

24
00:01:46.222 --> 00:01:52.420
Meta gen, list, probe runs every generator against this model, and shows what each one would emit.

25
00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:57.706
It takes what the job needs: entity, queries, routes, and a barrel.

26
00:01:57.706 --> 00:02:03.217
Meta eject copies each one into your repo — plain TypeScript generators, yours to own and edit.

27
00:02:03.217 --> 00:02:06.466
Forms and grids wait in the catalog until you want them.

28
00:02:07.866 --> 00:02:13.825
Claude wires them into the config, installs what they import, and runs meta gen.

29
00:02:13.825 --> 00:02:16.283
From that one model: seven files.

30
00:02:16.283 --> 00:02:22.243
Your database schema, validation, and a full CRUD API — none of it hand-written.

31
00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:27.199
And it's real, idiomatic code — Drizzle and Zod here.

32
00:02:27.199 --> 00:02:30.621
Not boilerplate you babysit; output you regenerate.

33
00:02:30.621 --> 00:02:34.512
Hand-edit a file and a three-way merge keeps your changes.

34
00:02:35.912 --> 00:02:44.322
Create the tables with meta migrate, boot the server, and it's a working app — the generated routes serving real task data over HTTP.

35
00:02:45.722 --> 00:02:49.021
The filters are generated and enforced from the model, too.

36
00:02:49.021 --> 00:02:51.648
Filterable fields work; anything else is a 400.

37
00:02:51.648 --> 00:02:53.158
No hand-written validation.

38
00:02:54.558 --> 00:02:56.304
Now the part that matters.

39
00:02:56.304 --> 00:03:02.012
Claude renames a field in the model — due date becomes deadline — and skips meta gen.

40
00:03:02.012 --> 00:03:04.229
The model moved; the code didn't.

41
00:03:04.229 --> 00:03:07.855
One meta verify catches it: exit one, the build fails.

42
00:03:07.855 --> 00:03:10.988
Regenerate with meta gen, and it's green again.

43
00:03:10.988 --> 00:03:16.322
Drift fails the build instead of shipping — even with an agent writing the code.

44
00:03:17.722 --> 00:03:20.783
So — what did that one model actually replace?

45
00:03:20.783 --> 00:03:24.311
I counted it in the generated code and the migration.

46
00:03:24.311 --> 00:03:37.090
The three status values are written once, and spelled eight times in the output: a database CHECK, the Drizzle column and its check, a TypeScript type, three Zod schemas, and the form options.

47
00:03:37.090 --> 00:03:39.553
The title's length limit, four times.

48
00:03:39.553 --> 00:03:44.013
Change either in the model, and meta gen moves every copy together.

49
00:03:44.013 --> 00:03:47.207
The model is the asset; the code is regenerable.

50
00:03:48.607 --> 00:03:50.276
That's getting started.

51
00:03:50.276 --> 00:03:54.992
Install the CLI, run meta init, and your agent already knows how.

52
00:03:54.992 --> 00:03:57.314
Everything's at metaobjects.dev.

53
00:03:57.314 --> 00:03:58.765
Thanks for watching.

